**2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add Problem Details support + custom handler
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must come before endpoint mapping
app.UseExceptionHandler();

app.MapGet("/example/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    // Exceptions thrown here are caught globally — no try-catch needed
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id });
});

app.Run();
```

**How it works:**

- `AddExceptionHandler<T>()` registers your `IExceptionHandler` (available since .NET 8).
- `UseExceptionHandler()` adds middleware that catches all unhandled exceptions and routes them through your handler.
- The handler maps exception types → status codes and returns a consistent `application/problem+json` response.
- For 500s, `Detail` is set to `null` so stack traces/internals are never exposed.

No per-endpoint try-catch required — the middleware handles everything globally.
